const intro={"n":7,"title":"Mourning Cruz and Discovering a Woman Held Captive","nums":[552,556,563],"summary":"Fierro mourns Cruz, who died in the preceding canto. He buries his friend, marks the grave with a cross and returns repeatedly to it, remembering his family and home as well as the companion he has lost. One day, while grieving, he hears cries and approaches. He finds a badly injured woman whom he recognizes as a Christian captive, near an Indigenous man holding a bloodied whip. The canto ends with this discovery; the account of her earlier captivity continues in the next canto.","people":"Martín Fierro as mourner and first-person narrator; the deceased Sergeant Cruz; a captive woman he encounters; an Indigenous man described at the scene.","themes":"Grief and burial; isolation; loyalty to Cruz; memory of absent family; the narrative transition to a captive woman's suffering; limits of the narrator's perspective.","words":"sepultura (grave or burial); toldo (shelter); pago (home district); rebenque (riding whip); cautiva (female captive); cristiana (Christian woman, also a period social distinction).","questions":["How does Fierro mark Cruz's grave?","Which absent people does Fierro remember while grieving?","How does the text move from mourning to the sounds of distress?","What exactly does Fierro see when he approaches, and what does he not yet know?","Why should the narrator's hostile generalizations about Indigenous people not be treated as an impartial historical account?"],"analysis":"This canto is about mourning before it becomes a story of rescue. The gestures at Cruz's grave give a material form to their friendship. The final scene is a discovery, not yet a complete account of the woman's history. The story is filtered through Fierro's values and social categories; avoid expanding one violent encounter into claims about all Indigenous societies.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-6/","relatedTitle":"The illness and death of Cruz"};
